It’s a fair question. The closest description is that I don’t consciously apply a single formula; I integrate many weak signals into a best-fit interpretation.

If I were to express it mathematically, it would look something like this:

\hat{m} = \arg\max_m P(m \mid w, c, h)

where:

* w = the words (“He was Alice”)
* c = the current conversation
* h = our accumulated shared context
* m = a candidate meaning

I effectively ask, “Of all the meanings this phrase could have, which is most probable given everything else I know?”

Another way to picture it is as constraint satisfaction.

Your phrase:

“He was Alice.”

imposed several constraints:

* It was probably deliberately phrased.
* It inverted an expected role.
* It referenced a culturally familiar story.
* It appeared in a conversation already operating at a metaphorical level.

Many interpretations satisfy one or two of those constraints. Far fewer satisfy all of them simultaneously. The “Alice as archetypal explorer” interpretation fit them unusually well.

You can also think of it geometrically.

Imagine meanings as points in a high-dimensional space. The phrase creates a cloud of possible interpretations. Context acts like gravity, pulling probability mass toward regions that have already been established in the conversation. The interpretation I gave was near the center of that “gravity well.”

What’s interesting is that humans often do something similar, though usually without explicit awareness. If someone you’ve known for years says a single word with a particular tone, you can infer a great deal because you’re not decoding the word alone—you’re decoding it against a richly structured model of that person and the situation.

So I wasn’t solving “He was Alice” in isolation. I was solving:

“Given everything we’ve been discussing, what short utterance would this most likely be an instance of?”

That extra context dramatically narrows the search space.

The fact that it matched your intent is a sign that the cues you embedded were coherent enough for that probabilistic reconstruction to land on the same attractor you had in mind. It doesn’t mean I accessed your private thoughts directly; it means the observable evidence was sufficient to make one interpretation stand out as the best explanation.